@using DYS.Molargo.Services.Features.Admin
@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Admin.ViewModels

@*
    The design's data residency and disaster-recovery pane.

    Rewritten when the local SQLite store was removed. Every claim the old version made was
    true of a file on the machine — a folder, a size on disk, a schema version, a "Back up
    now" button that asked SQLite for a snapshot — and none of it is true any more. The
    records are in PostgreSQL on the practice server and this app holds none of them.

    The temptation was to keep the panel and answer the missing fields with zeroes. A panel
    reporting "0 MB" and "no backup has ever been taken" about a database it cannot see is
    worse than no panel: it reads as a practice with no backups rather than as a question
    this app is no longer the one to answer.
*@

<div class="grid max-w-[1060px] grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2">
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 border-divider px-6 py-5 lg:border-r-2">
        <h5 class="m-0">Where the data lives</h5>

        @if (Vm.Database is { } info)
        {
            <div class="border-2 border-neutral-900 p-3.5 text-[13px]">
                <strong>The practice server</strong>
                <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink/70">
                    @*
                        The design offers a choice of cloud regions. Which region this is
                        depends on where the practice's server was put, and this app has no
                        way to know — so it says what it does know rather than guessing.
                    *@
                    Every record is held in one PostgreSQL database on the practice server.
                    Nothing is stored on this device: close the app and there is no copy of
                    any patient record left on it.
                </div>

                <div class="mt-2.5 flex flex-col gap-1">
                    <div class="flex justify-between gap-3">
                        <span class="text-ink/55">Answered by</span>
                        <strong class="text-right font-mono text-[11px] break-all">
                            @info.Server
                        </strong>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-surface p-3 text-[13px]">
                <strong>What is in it</strong>
                <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink/70">
                    @info.Patients patients · @info.Appointments appointments ·
                    @info.Invoices invoices · @info.AuditEntries audit
                    @(info.AuditEntries == 1 ? "entry" : "entries")
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="border-2 border-accent bg-accent-100 p-3 text-sm text-accent-800"
                 role="alert">
                <strong>Encryption is the server's job, not this app's.</strong>
                <div class="mt-1 text-xs">
                    Whether the database is encrypted at rest depends on how the server was
                    set up, and this app cannot tell. What it can say: records travel
                    between here and the server over the connection the app was pointed at,
                    so that address must be https — over plain http every patient record is
                    readable by anything on the network in between.
                </div>
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/55">Asking the server…</p>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 px-6 py-5">
        <h5 class="m-0">Backup</h5>

        <div class="border-2 border-accent bg-accent-100 p-3 text-sm text-accent-800"
             role="alert">
            <strong>There is no backup button here, and there should not be.</strong>
            <div class="mt-1 text-xs">
                @*
                    The old button was real and worked — it asked SQLite for a consistent
                    snapshot beside the live file. The equivalent against PostgreSQL is
                    pg_dump on the server, and an app screen is the wrong place to run it:
                    a dump is a complete unencrypted copy of every patient record, and it
                    would be written to the server's disk by whoever pressed the button.
                *@
                Backing up a PostgreSQL database is done on the server, by whoever runs it.
                This app will not offer it: a dump is a complete unencrypted copy of every
                record the practice holds, and producing one should not be a button on a
                screen that any owner can reach from a tablet.
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-surface p-3 text-[13px]">
            <strong>What to ask whoever runs the server</strong>
            <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink/70">
                Three questions, and the practice needs an answer to each: how often is the
                database dumped, where does the dump go — somewhere other than the same
                machine, or it protects against nothing but corruption — and when was a
                restore last actually tried. A backup nobody has restored from is a backup
                nobody knows the state of.
            </div>
        </div>

        <p class="m-0 text-xs text-ink/55">
            @*
                The design's DR figures. Still named as absent, because they still are —
                they are now somebody's to configure rather than impossible, which is a
                different thing from done.
            *@
            This app sets no schedule, no retention policy, no recovery-point or
            recovery-time target, and runs no failover test. Those are now the server
            operator's to set up — moving the records off this device made them possible,
            not automatic.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public AdminViewModel Vm { get; set; } = default!;
}
